I don't think this is talking about entitlement at all, actually; people
are entitled to post under any name they want (and probably people
shouldn't pass themselves off as someone they're not, in the sense of
using the pseudonym or real name of another person/poster), but it's
basically impossible to prevent a sufficiently determined/resourced
person from finding the posts you've made on Usenet if you make them
under your real name.

Isn't that just realism, rather than any sort of "chilling effect"? It's
basically not *possible*, as usenet stands, to have both
anonymity-wrt-search and to use one's real name. Maybe a protocol with
better properties in this respect will become popular, and supplant
usenet (it may not even be possible: I'm no security wonk, and I'm sure
signal analysis of postings is entirely possible even if the contents
are inaccessible). X-no-archive is a band-aid: it prevents casual
searching, but not anyone who's either a) genuinely nasty (who in the
extreme case can just archive all of Usenet and ignore it) or b)
determined enough to piece together the gist of what someone's saying
from the quoting of their posts.

(Anyone who wants to dejagoogle me will find amongst other things I like
Go, Nethack, and NFL football - specifically the Patriots. I'm not
especially bothered by people knowing this, so I use my given name.
Anyone else can make whatever decision best suits them as to what name
to use, and I'll respect it.)

